<main class="mx-auto flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-16 px-6 py-12" data-role="game-page">
  <header class="flex flex-col gap-5">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-primary">Thereish</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      Two far-apart words. One bridge. How close did you land?
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg opacity-80">
      A couch word game for two to six phones and a laptop. Everyone writes the word
      that spans the gap, and a machine grades how close the table came. No accounts,
      nothing to install.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <.link href={~p"/new"} class="btn btn-primary" data-role="game-new-room">Open a Room</.link>
      <.link href={~p"/"} class="btn btn-soft">I have a Code</.link>
    </div>
  </header>

  <figure class="flex flex-col gap-2">
    <video
      src={~p"/images/game/stage-page.mp4"}
      poster={~p"/images/game/stage-meld.webp"}
      autoplay
      muted
      loop
      playsinline
      controls
      preload="metadata"
      class="w-full rounded-box border border-base-300"
      aria-label="A whole Page on the Stage: longship and defibrillator miss at 14, then oars and rowing meld at 94."
    ></video>
    <figcaption class="text-sm opacity-60">
      One whole Page, cut to 28 seconds: <em>longship</em>
      and <em>defibrillator</em>
      miss at 14, then <em>oars</em>
      and <em>rowing</em>
      meld at 94.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-6">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">How a Page goes</h2>
    <ol class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-mono text-2xl font-bold text-primary">1</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Open a Room.</strong>
          It gets a four-letter Code. Read it aloud; everyone else types it and a name.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-mono text-2xl font-bold text-primary">2</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Everyone gives a Give</strong>
          — a setting in a phrase, like “Viking beaches” or “a hospital night shift.”
          It stays secret until the reveal.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-mono text-2xl font-bold text-primary">3</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Two Bookends appear,</strong>
          written in seconds from two different Gives and chosen to sit far apart.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-mono text-2xl font-bold text-primary">4</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Everyone writes a Bridge:</strong>
          the word or phrase whose meaning spans both. Then the reveal: every Bridge,
          one number, and a one-line quip about what you actually wrote.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <figure class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
        <img
          src={~p"/images/game/phone-give.webp"}
          width="540"
          height="709"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="A phone showing Room Code FANO and the Give 'Viking beaches'."
          class="w-full max-w-72 rounded-box border border-base-300"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-sm opacity-60">Your Give, kept private.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
        <img
          src={~p"/images/game/phone-bridge.webp"}
          width="540"
          height="624"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="A phone showing the Bookends longship and defibrillator and a box for your Bridge."
          class="w-full max-w-72 rounded-box border border-base-300"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-sm opacity-60">Two Bookends, one box.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-5">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">The Ladder</h2>
    <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2" data-role="game-ladder">
      <li :for={{band, floor} <- ladder()} class="badge badge-lg badge-soft">
        {band} <span class="font-semibold text-primary">{floor}</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A Meld ends the Page. On a miss, the two Bridges that landed furthest apart
      become the next Bookends, so every Attempt starts closer than the last. Five
      Attempts, then the Page closes, melded or not. “One more?” asks for fresh Gives.
    </p>
    <figure class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <img
        src={~p"/images/game/stage-miss.webp"}
        width="1280"
        height="800"
        loading="lazy"
        alt="The Stage: raid and paddles graded 14, a miss, with the quip: Ben brought the tools to row Ada's raid, though mostly to restart the crew's hearts afterward."
        class="w-full rounded-box border border-base-300"
      />
      <figcaption class="text-sm opacity-60">
        A miss is often the best part. The number is the punchline.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-5">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What you need</h2>
    <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <li class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-4">
        <p class="font-semibold">Two to six phones</p>
        <p class="text-sm opacity-70">One per player, as a private screen.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-4">
        <p class="font-semibold">A laptop, if you like</p>
        <p class="text-sm opacity-70">
          Make it the Stage and the reveal plays there for the whole table.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-4">
        <p class="font-semibold">About ten minutes</p>
        <p class="text-sm opacity-70">A Page ends cleanly. One more is a single tap.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-5">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">The machine keeps score, not a seat</h2>
    <p>
      Language models write the Bookends, grade the Bridges, write the quips, and
      keep the Room's Chronicle: every Page, every grade and its reasoning, and a
      short recap after you've gone to bed. They never play. Every player is a
      person at the table, and if you all agree a grade was wrong, you can overrule it.
      The same Bridges get the same grade forever.
    </p>
    <figure class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <img
        src={~p"/images/game/stage-meld.webp"}
        width="1280"
        height="800"
        loading="lazy"
        alt="The Stage: oars and rowing graded 94, a Meld, with the quip: Longships or hospital wards, you two have finally agreed on how to propel the boat."
        class="w-full rounded-box border border-base-300"
      />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col items-start gap-4 rounded-box bg-base-200 p-6">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Play tonight</h2>
    <p>Open a Room on the laptop, make it the Stage, and read the Code to the room.</p>
    <.link href={~p"/new"} class="btn btn-primary">Open a Room</.link>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-70">
      How it was built, and why:
      <a
        href="https://stephens.page/blog/the-machine-grades-it-never-rules/"
        class="underline underline-offset-2"
      >
        The machine grades. It never rules.
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <Layouts.made_by />
</main>
